# Programs of this corpus whose output is not a function of this tree.
# One basename per line, each with the reason -- where the program gets
# what this tree cannot supply, named specifically enough that a later
# reader can go and look.
#
# The entry says what a difference may be read as, not what any two
# runs will be. A program that draws on the clock is free to give the
# same answer twice, and how often it does is a property of the machine
# rather than of the tree, so nothing here is measured by rendering the
# program again and seeing whether it moved: a check like that reports
# the machine's jitter and reddens on a run that found none. What the
# entry rests on is the reason below it, and the corpus fails for an
# entry that carries none -- a bare name excuses every difference the
# program ever shows and says nothing about why, which is the one thing
# it was for.
#
# vdp -- prints how long it took onto the page it is timing. The
#        program is a demonstration of caching a costly drawing in a
#        form, and it shows its working: it takes realtime before and
#        after each stage and shows the differences as text at the head
#        of every page ("About to start plotting ... at runtime N",
#        "Took N ms"). realtime counts in real time independently of
#        the interpreter's execution (PLRM 8.2, realtime), so those
#        digits are how busy the machine was while it drew, and they
#        are painted in the same ink as the barcodes underneath them.
#        The rest of the page is this tree's and is compared with it.
vdp
